 ## Background
-The problem of joint pose and NeRF optimization emerged as a solution to the dependency on accurate Structure-from-Motion (SfM) pipelines, which can fail in low-texture or dynamic scenes. Early work by Yen-Chen et al. (iNeRF, December 2020) formulated pose reconstruction as an inverse problem, optimizing pose using photometric loss on a pre-trained NeRF [S1]. Subsequently, NeRF– (February 2021) jointly estimated NeRF model parameters and camera parameters, allowing for end-to-end radiance field construction comparable to COLMAP baselines [S1]. Jeong et al. introduced SCNeRF (August 2021), which extended this to self-calibration of intrinsic parameters for non-linear models like fisheye lenses using curriculum learning [S1]. These methods collectively established the foundation for differentiable inverse rendering where camera parameters are treated as learnable variables alongside scene geometry.
+The problem of joint pose and NeRF optimization emerged as a solution to the dependency on accurate Structure-from-Motion (SfM) pipelines like COLMAP, which can fail on textureless or repetitive scenes. Early work by Yen-Chen et al. (iNeRF, December 2020) formulated pose reconstruction as an inverse problem, optimizing pose using photometric loss on a pre-trained NeRF [S1]. Subsequently, NeRF– (February 2021) jointly estimated NeRF model parameters and camera parameters, allowing end-to-end construction of radiance fields [S1]. Jeong et al. introduced SCNeRF (August 2021), which extended this to self-calibration of intrinsic parameters for non-linear models like fisheye lenses using curriculum learning [S1]. Other approaches like GNeRF (March 2021) used adversarial training with pose as a generative latent code [S1]. More recent methods address specific challenges such as dynamic scenes (RoDynRF), continuous motion (Time-dependent NeRF), and preconditioning for ill-conditioned optimization (CamP) [S1].
 
